{
  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component attempts good accessibility fundamentals with semantic list elements and a persistent `role=\"status\"` live region. However, it contains severe anti-patterns: `display: none` on the `<label>` completely removes it from the accessibility tree, `aria-label` improperly overrides the label semantics, search query state transitions cause immediate false announcements, and there is no focus management or landmark structure to guide keyboard or screen reader users to the results.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\n- **Prediction 1**: Live region re-renders or mounting issues causing dropped announcements in assistive tech. *(Actual: The live region container is persistent in DOM which is good, but inner text clearing and state logic causes an instant false \"No results found\" announcement when typing or submitting before completion).*\n- **Prediction 2**: Incomplete form control labeling or conflicting ARIA name properties. *(Actual: Confirmed \u2014 `<label>` has `display: none`, hiding it from AT, while `aria-label` clobbers the label text).*\n- **Prediction 3**: Focus remains trapped or stranded on the submit button with no mechanism to navigate quickly to the new results list. *(Actual: Confirmed \u2014 no skip mechanism, no heading focus movement, and no programmatic relationship linking input/form to results).*\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n1. **Hidden form label using `display: none` completely removes it from the accessibility tree**\n   - **Code**: `SearchResults.css:12` (`.search-container label { display: none; }`) & `SearchResults.jsx:31`\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader and voice-control users.\n   - **WCAG / APG citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), WCAG 3.3.2 (Labels or Instructions).\n   - **Why this matters**: CSS `display: none` removes the element from the accessibility tree in all major browsers/screen readers. The developer intended to visually hide the label, but instead used `display: none` instead of the `.sr-only` utility class. Although `aria-label=\"Search query\"` was added to the input, native labels must be accessible, visible labels should exist for cognitive/speech users, and `display: none` on `<label htmlFor=\"...\">` breaks standard programmatic association.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Fix**: Remove `display: none` from `.search-container label`. If visually hiding the label is desired (though a visible label is strongly recommended per WCAG 3.3.2), apply the existing `.sr-only` class to `<label>`: `<label htmlFor=\"search-input\" className=\"sr-only\">Search</label>`.\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n1. **Flawed status state logic triggers premature \"No results found\" announcement**\n   - **Code**: `SearchResults.jsx:48` (`{!isLoading && results.length === 0 && query && 'No results found'}`)\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG / APG citation**: WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages).\n   - **Why this matters**: When the user submits a search (`handleSearch`), `setIsLoading(true)` and `setResults([])` are called. However, before the user submits, or if query is typed and results are empty, the live region immediately computes `query && results.length === 0` as true and announces \"No results found\" before the user even presses Enter or while the form is fresh. Furthermore, when `isLoading` toggles, screen readers can catch conflicting updates.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Fix**: Decouple the status announcement from real-time `query` state. Maintain an explicit `statusMessage` state or track search execution lifecycle (e.g. `hasSearched` flag):\n     ```jsx\n     {isLoading && 'Searching...'}\n     {!isLoading && hasSearched && results.length > 0 && `Found ${results.length} results`}\n     {!isLoading && hasSearched && results.length === 0 && `No results found for \"${submittedQuery}\"`}\n     ```\n\n2. **Redundant and conflicting accessible name (`aria-label` overriding `<label>`)**\n   - **Code**: `SearchResults.jsx:38` (`aria-label=\"Search query\"` vs `<label htmlFor=\"search-input\">Search</label>`)\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users, speech-input users (Voice Control).\n   - **WCAG / APG citation**: WCAG 2.5.3 (Label in Name), WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value).\n   - **Why this matters**: When an input has both `<label>` and `aria-label`, the `aria-label` overrides the visible label in the accessible name computation. If the visible label says \"Search\" but the accessible name is \"Search query\", speech-input users who say \"Click Search\" will experience command failure or mismatch.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Fix**: Remove `aria-label=\"Search query\"` from `<input>`. Rely purely on `<label htmlFor=\"search-input\">Search</label>`.\n\n3. **No landmark structure or programmatic connection between search and results**\n   - **Code**: `SearchResults.jsx:29`, `54`\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader and keyboard-only users.\n   - **WCAG / APG citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order).\n   - **Why this matters**: The search interface uses a bare generic `<div>`. Screen reader users navigating by landmarks cannot jump to `<search>` or `<section aria-label=\"Search Results\">`. Keyboard users who activate the search button stay focused on `<button type=\"submit\">Search</button>` with no prompt, skip link, or focus transition to reach the newly rendered results without manually tabbing past any potential future controls.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Fix**: \n     1. Wrap the form in a native `<search>` landmark (or `<div role=\"search\">` for older browsers).\n     2. Wrap results in `<section aria-labelledby=\"results-heading\">` with `<h2 id=\"results-heading\">Search Results</h2>`.\n     3. Consider adding `aria-controls=\"search-results\"` on the search form/button and `id=\"search-results\"` on the results container.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings & Enhancements\n\n- **Lack of visible focus styling (`:focus-visible`)**:\n  - `SearchResults.css:17-30`: Input and button do not define high-contrast, prominent `:focus-visible` styles. Default browser focus outlines may be suppressed or faint against the `#0066cc` border/background. (WCAG 2.4.7, 2.4.13).\n- **Missing `aria-busy=\"true\"` on the results region during loading**:\n  - While `role=\"status\"` announces the change, marking the container or form with `aria-busy={isLoading}` informs assistive tech that the dynamic widget is in transition. (WCAG 4.1.2).\n- **Sub-optimal color contrast on placeholder and secondary text**:\n  - `SearchResults.css:84`: `#666` on `#ffffff` provides a contrast ratio of ~4.54:1 (barely passing 4.5:1). On lower contrast screens or small sizes, this can be hard to read. Recommend darkening secondary text to `#555` (~7.0:1) or `#444`.\n\n---\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Hidden label via `display: none`, false status announcements, name clobbering |\n| Keyboard-only | **MEDIUM** | Focus stays on submit button with no direct skip/jump to results |\n| Low vision | **MEDIUM** | Hidden visible label, reliance on placeholder text, border contrast |\n| Cognitive | **MEDIUM** | Disappearing placeholder, absence of persistent visible label |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No animations or transitions present |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No audio/video elements |\n| Environmental contrast | **MEDIUM** | `#666` secondary text near 4.5:1 boundary |\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Summary\n\n- **Screen Reader User**: Severely impacted by the CSS `display: none` on `<label>`. While `aria-label` provides an accessible name fallback, the conflicting values create confusion. The live region will announce \"No results found\" prematurely whenever characters are in the input before searching.\n- **Keyboard-only User**: The interaction is operable (form submits via Enter/Space), but once submitted, focus remains stuck on the submit button. Users must tab forward to reach the results list.\n- **Low Vision & Cognitive Users**: Hiding the label entirely leaves only the `placeholder=\"Search for...\"`, which disappears as soon as typing begins. Visible labels are essential for cognitive accessibility and speech recognition users.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n\nThe component is rated **REVISE**. The basic intent to use `role=\"status\"` and semantic `<ul>/<li>` elements is good, but:\n1. Using `display: none` on `<label>` breaks the primary accessible relationship.\n2. Incomplete status logic triggers phantom \"No results found\" speech events.\n3. The lack of `<search>` landmarks and visible label hurts multiple user groups.\n\nFixing the label styling (`.sr-only` or visible label), removing redundant `aria-label`, and guarding the status messages will elevate this component to **ACCEPT**.\n",
